 function ${y}_{c}(x)$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-2x{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nLet $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-2x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+2{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-2\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+2{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-2\\lambda +2] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-2\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =2.$\nTherefore, the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is proportional to ${x}^{3}.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is the constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=3\\alpha {x}^{2},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=6\\alpha x.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6\\alpha x)-2x(3\\alpha {x}^{2})+2(\\alpha {x}^{3}) & =2{x}^{3} \\\\ (6\\alpha -6\\alpha +2\\alpha ){x}^{3} & =2{x}^{3} \\\\ 2\\alpha {x}^{3} & =2{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get $\\alpha =1.$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)