_{1}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{t}\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3t}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t-6){e}^{t}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{3}{e}^{t} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{3}+t-2+\\frac{2}{t}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{3}){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(t-2+\\frac{2}{t}){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535880 true] [17592186152545 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535880 true] [17592186152545 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$x^{2}y^{″} + xy^{′} - 4y = 6x, x > 0$." 13194139535880 false] [17592186152545 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-4y=6x,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139535880 true] [17592186152545 1145 "First, we need to find the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-4y=0.\n$$\n\nLet $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -4] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =2.$ Thus, the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{2}}+{c}_{2}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is proportional to $6x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x\n$