bda =1$ and $\\lambda =3.$ Thus, the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is proportional to ${t}^{4}.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {t}^{4}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is the constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=4\\alpha {t}^{3},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=12\\alpha {t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}(12\\alpha {t}^{2})-3t(4\\alpha {t}^{3})+3(\\alpha {t}^{4}) & =-{t}^{4} \\\\ (12\\alpha -12\\alpha +3\\alpha ){t}^{4} & =-{t}^{4} \\\\ 3\\alpha {t}^{4} & =-{t}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372086 13194139535880 true] [17592186372085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372087 13194139535880 true] [17592186372085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372088 13194139535880 true] [17592186372085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372089 13194139535880 true] [17592186372085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372090 13194139535880 true] [17592186372085 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535880 true] [17592186372085 1041 "selection" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372085 1104 #edb/ref 17592186372086 13194139535880 true] [17592186372085 1110 #uuid "bebbebc3-837b-586b-8a78-fe64c15d475c" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372086 1034 "y(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{3}" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372086 1103 #edb/ref 1759218