 $x={e}^{t}.$*" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372175 1113 #uuid "3f0ce560-21d3-5ff3-86e5-dadb1ef0133b" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372175 1145 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y=t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{t}+Bt{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-2\\alpha +(\\alpha t+\\beta )=t+2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha t+\\beta -2\\alpha =t+2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ \\beta -2\\alpha =2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants}).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =4,$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t+4.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4.\n$$" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372177 13194139535880 true] [17592186372176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372178 13194139535880 true] [17592186372176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372179 13194139535880 true] [17592186372176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372180 13194139535880 true] [17592186372176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372181 13194139535880 true] [17592186372176 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535880 true] [17592186372176 1041 "selection" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372176 1104 #edb/ref 17592186372179 13194139535880 true] [17592186372176 1110 #uuid "0f6c41d8-96ec-523a-af3e-efdaad776007" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372177 1034 "{y}_{p}(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372177 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535880 true] [17592186372177 1111 #uuid "76aa7a5f-a3a8-51ed-b7aa-5017f45498cd" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372178 1034 "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535880 true] [17592186372178 1111 #uuid "d8f8fe5b-b494-5376-ad79-2a240c2baaa2" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372179 1034 "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372179 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535880 true] [17592186372179 1111 #uuid "6fe61163-7858-53ec-b4d4-4caa457f0885" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372180 1034 "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372180 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535880 true] [17592186372180 1111 #uuid "a21a6d21-e940-5eaf-a37a-21b5d47af771" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372181 1034 "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535880 true] [17592186372181 1111 #uuid "1d3cc830-0a9b-586f-a018-f16048f2f86b" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372182 1017 #edb/ref 17592186372183 13194139535880 true] [17592186372182 1089 "q-94226" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372182 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535880 true] [17592186372182 1100 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+3y=x{e}^{x},\\,x>0\n$$\n\nhas the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(x)=\\frac{A}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{B}{x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. \n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:* \n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{x}\\text{d}x=({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6){e}^{x}+C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{x}\\text{d}x=(x-1){e}^{x}+C\n$$" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372182 1113 #uuid "a237827c-e559-5ab9-ade9-5d51e7b60d0a" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372182 1145 "First, we reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nSo, we divide both sides of the given equation by ${x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5}{x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe notice that the forcing term $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′}-{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}^{′}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})-(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6){e}^{x}, \\\\ v(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\int x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}(x-1){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})(-\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6){e}^{x})+(\\frac{1}{x})(\\frac{1}{2}(x-1){e}^{x}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2x}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2x}){e}^{x} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535880 true] [17592186372183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372184 13194139535880 true] [17592186372183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372185 13194139535880 true] [17592186372183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372186 13194139535880 true]